        "9": {
          "id": "9",
          "name": "bigfoot.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n\n    <a class=\"back-link\" href=\"index.html\">Back to Mystical Creatures</a>\n\n    <h1>Bigfoot</h1>\n\n    <div class=\"page-image\">\n      <img src=\"bigfoot-photo.jpg\" alt=\"A wooden carving of Bigfoot standing in a snowy forest\">\n    </div>\n\n    <p>Bigfoot, also known as Sasquatch, is said to be a large, hairy, ape-like creature that roams the forests of the Pacific Northwest in the United States and Canada. Descriptions of the creature vary, but most accounts describe it as walking upright like a human, standing anywhere from six to nine feet tall, and covered in dark hair.</p>\n\n    <p>Stories of a \"wild man of the woods\" have roots in the oral traditions of many Indigenous peoples of the Pacific Northwest long before the name \"Bigfoot\" was popularized in the 1950s. Since then, thousands of alleged sightings, footprint casts, and blurry photographs have kept the legend alive, even though no physical evidence has ever been scientifically confirmed to belong to an unknown species.</p>\n\n    <p>Skeptics point out that most reported evidence can be explained by known animals, hoaxes, or misidentification, while believers argue that the sheer number of consistent eyewitness accounts across such a wide area is hard to dismiss. Either way, Bigfoot remains one of the most enduring legends in North American folklore, inspiring everything from serious wildlife expeditions to roadside statues like the one pictured here.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"citation\">\n      <p>Photo by <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/@jonmsailer?utm_source=unsplash&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_content=creditCopyText\">Jon Sailer</a> on <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/a-person-with-a-beard-vt9v8arVaQs?utm_source=unsplash&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_content=creditCopyText\">Unsplash</a></p>\n    </div>\n\n  </body>\n</html>\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "10": {
          "id": "10",
          "name": "dragon.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n\n    <a class=\"back-link\" href=\"index.html\">Back to Mystical Creatures</a>\n\n    <h1>Dragon</h1>\n\n    <div class=\"page-image\">\n      <img src=\"dragon-illustration.jpg\" alt=\"A line-art illustration of a large winged dragon\">\n    </div>\n\n    <p>Dragons are among the most widespread mythical creatures in the world, appearing in the folklore of nearly every continent, though their depictions differ dramatically depending on the culture telling the story.</p>\n\n    <p>In European folklore, dragons are typically shown as large, fire-breathing, winged reptiles that guard treasure and must be defeated by a brave knight or hero. In contrast, dragons in Chinese and other East Asian traditions are usually wingless, serpentine, and associated with wisdom, water, and good fortune rather than danger and destruction.</p>\n\n    <p>Historians believe dragon myths may have been influenced by the discovery of dinosaur and other large animal fossils long before paleontology existed as a science, giving ancient peoples a way to explain enormous bones they couldn't otherwise account for. Today, dragons remain a defining figure in fantasy stories, games, and art, much like the illustration above, which draws on the classic Western image of a scaled, winged dragon.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"citation\">\n      <p>By LadyofHats, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DnD_Dragon.png\">Wikimedia Commons</a></p>\n    </div>\n\n  </body>\n</html>\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "11": {
          "id": "11",
          "name": "griffin.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n\n    <a class=\"back-link\" href=\"index.html\">Back to Mystical Creatures</a>\n\n    <h1>Griffin</h1>\n\n    <div class=\"page-image\">\n      <img src=\"griffin-illustration.jpg\" alt=\"A historical engraving of a griffin, part eagle and part lion\">\n    </div>\n\n    <p>The griffin is a legendary creature with the body, tail, and back legs of a lion, combined with the head, wings, and talons of an eagle. Because the lion and the eagle were both seen as kings within their own domains, the griffin came to represent a powerful guardian, said to watch over gold, treasure, and other priceless possessions.</p>\n\n    <p>Stories of griffins appear in the mythology of ancient Greece, Persia, and the Near East, often describing them as fierce protectors of mountain passes or hidden riches, sometimes even said to build nests lined with gold. Griffins were also common figures in medieval European heraldry, appearing on coats of arms and family crests as a symbol of courage, strength, and vigilance.</p>\n\n    <p>Some historians think griffin legends may have been inspired by ancient people discovering fossilized dinosaur skeletons, such as those of the beaked, four-legged Protoceratops, in regions along historic trade routes. The engraving above, created in the 1600s, reflects how artists of that era pictured the griffin as a very real, almost scientifically documented animal.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"citation\">\n      <p>Engraved by Matthäus Merian, 1660, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Griffioen.jpg\">Wikimedia Commons</a></p>\n    </div>\n\n  </body>\n</html>\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "13": {
          "id": "13",
          "name": "kraken.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n\n    <a class=\"back-link\" href=\"index.html\">Back to Mystical Creatures</a>\n\n    <h1>Kraken</h1>\n\n    <div class=\"page-image\">\n      <img src=\"kraken-illustration.jpg\" alt=\"A historical engraving of a giant kraken attacking a sailing ship\">\n    </div>\n\n    <p>The kraken is a legendary sea monster said to lurk in the icy waters near Norway and Greenland, large enough to be mistaken for an island and powerful enough to drag entire ships beneath the waves. Sailors' tales of the kraken date back centuries, appearing in Scandinavian folklore as early as the 1700s.</p>\n\n    <p>Descriptions of the kraken vary widely, from a colossal crab-like creature to something resembling a massive octopus or squid, complete with enormous tentacles capable of wrapping around a ship's hull. Some accounts even claimed that the kraken's presence could be detected by a swirling whirlpool it created when it dove back underwater.</p>\n\n    <p>Many historians believe sightings of real giant squid, which can grow to over 40 feet long and were rarely seen alive until modern deep-sea exploration, likely inspired these legends. The illustration above, created in the early 1800s, reflects just how vividly people imagined this creature long before science could confirm what giant squid actually look like.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"citation\">\n      <p>By Pierre Denys de Montfort, 1801, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denys_de_Montfort_Poulpe_Colossal.jpg\">Wikimedia Commons</a></p>\n    </div>\n\n  </body>\n</html>\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "14": {
          "id": "14",
          "name": "phoenix.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n\n    <a class=\"back-link\" href=\"index.html\">Back to Mystical Creatures</a>\n\n    <h1>Phoenix</h1>\n\n    <div class=\"page-image\">\n      <img src=\"phoenix-illustration.jpg\" alt=\"A historical illustration of a phoenix rising from flames\">\n    </div>\n\n    <p>The phoenix is a mythical bird said to live for hundreds of years before bursting into flames and being reborn from its own ashes. Because of this cycle, the phoenix has become one of the most widely recognized symbols of renewal, hope, and immortality across many different cultures.</p>\n\n    <p>The legend is often traced back to ancient Egypt, where the phoenix was linked to the sun god Ra and the sacred city of Heliopolis. From there, the story spread to Greek and Roman mythology, and eventually became a common symbol throughout Christian and Western art and literature. Some scholars also see similarities between the phoenix and mythical firebirds found in Chinese and Slavic folklore, suggesting these stories may share common roots.</p>\n\n    <p>Unlike creatures said to be spotted in remote forests or oceans, the phoenix is understood by nearly everyone as a purely symbolic being rather than a claim about a real animal. Even so, its story of destruction followed by rebirth has made it one of the most enduring and widely referenced myths in the world, still showing up today in literature, logos, and popular culture.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"citation\">\n      <p>By Friedrich Justin Bertuch, 1806, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phoenix-Fabelwesen.jpg\">Wikimedia Commons</a></p>\n    </div>\n\n  </body>\n</html>\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "15": {
          "id": "15",
          "name": "style.css",
          "contents": "/* 1) Add a rule set to style the images with the hover pseudo-class */\n\n\n\n/* basic style for all text */\nbody {\n  font-family: Verdana;\n  text-align: center;\n  font-weight: bold;\n  background-color: azure;\n}\n\n/* flex layout for images in container */\n.images {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: row;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n  justify-content: space-evenly;\n  align-content: center;\n}\n\n/* basic image style */\nimg {\n  width: 250px;\n  border-style: inset;\n  border-color:darkmagenta;\n  border-radius: 5px;\n}\n\n/* styles for the new creature pages (bigfoot.html, kraken.html,\n   phoenix.html, dragon.html, griffin.html, unicorn.html) */\n.back-link {\n  margin: 15px;\n  font-family: Verdana;\n  font-size: 16px;\n  color: darkmagenta;\n  text-decoration: none;\n}\n\n.back-link:hover {\n  text-decoration: underline;\n}\n\n.page-image {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n}\n\n.page-image img {\n  width: 400px;\n}",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": true
        },
